{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2549","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2549","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2549\",\"parent_revision\":\"c9a1efca4fe19333bdc8728bf1aef262eaa1bbfb91e44b318ff1a411a1bac8b5\",\"root_revision\":\"c9a1efca4fe19333bdc8728bf1aef262eaa1bbfb91e44b318ff1a411a1bac8b5\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nFollow-up to the startup background cache-sync feature (PR #2546). The P1 retry-classification regression is already fixed on that branch; this item collects the remaining review findings plus a broader offline-first capability. The unifying problem: the harness should let work continue against the local cache while the backend is unavailable, and converge cleanly when it returns \u2014 rather than treating backend-unavailable as a blocking error.\\n\\nObserved problems (PR #2546 review, chatgpt-codex-connector, 2026-06-03):\\n\\n1. Non-GitHub backends forced OFFLINE. The startup sync calls refresh_local_cache_from_github unconditionally. beads/sqlite backends return None from try_get_github, so every startup raises BackendUnavailableError \u2192 OFFLINE and backlog_list emits stale-cache warnings even when the configured backend is healthy. (sync_engine.py startup path)\\n\\n2. Read-path loses stale-cache fallback. backlog_list(from_github=True) now propagates the backend-unavailable raise through list_items, so the tool returns an error instead of serving the cached items. Before the offline-propagation change this path warned and continued. The offline contract is to serve stale cache on reads. (operations.py list_items / server.backlog_list)\\n\\n3. GitHub 403 primary rate-limit classified non-retryable. GitHub can return HTTP 403 with x-ratelimit-remaining: 0 and no Retry-After for a primary limit; classify_sync_error currently treats 403-without-Retry-After as non-retryable, so a transient primary-limit response enters OFFLINE instead of bounded retry. (sync_state._classify_github_exception)\\n\\n4. Lifespan re-entry gaps. After the first startup sync completes (status IDLE), a re-entered lifespan passes try_start() and launches a second sync; and when re-entered while the first is still running, the inner context is assigned the outer's task and its finally cancels that still-needed task. The once-per-process guard only holds while the first task is running. (server._backlog_lifespan)\\n\\nWhere observed: PR #2546 review threads (discussion ids r3346006811/13/16 and r3346074704/05/09). Verified no offline write-journal exists today: grep for journal|replay|write_ahead|offline_queue|pending_writes|outbox across backlog_core/*.py returned nothing (2026-06-03).\\n\\nImpact: non-GitHub deployments see false OFFLINE/stale warnings; an explicit refresh while offline returns an error instead of cached data; transient GitHub rate-limits skip the retry path; and edge-case lifespan re-entry can abort or duplicate the startup sync. Writes made while the backend is down are not captured anywhere and are lost.\\n\\n**User-provided context** (design intent / candidate directions for grooming \u2014 not requirements):\\n- Reads should always degrade to the local cache while the backend is unavailable, with the divergence/sync_state signal already present marking staleness.\\n- Writes made while offline should be journaled locally and replayed/reconciled against the live backend when it returns online (write-ahead log + reconcile on reconnect). Reuse the existing read-reconciliation logic (_pick_entry / _merge_entry_bodies in operations.py) for conflict resolution; drain the journal before the next refresh when the sync transitions OFFLINE\u2192online; design the journal schema, idempotency keys, ordering, and conflict policy during planning.\\n- Make the startup sync backend-aware so it no-ops (not OFFLINE) for backends that do not use the GitHub refresh path.\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-08)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:23:13.263859Z</sub>\\n\\n6/10 \u2014 Four concrete code-level defects with clear fixes and direct line-number evidence. Non-GitHub deployments (sqlite, beads) are affected on every server start. Priority is tempered by P1 item #2573 (in-progress) which absorbs backend-aware skip and the write-through model; after #2573 lands the scope of this item may reduce to three targeted fixes (read-path fallback, 403 retry, lifespan re-entry guard).\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:15:41.961042Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Blocks**: Non-GitHub backend users (sqlite, beads) see false OFFLINE warnings on every server start, degrading trust in the sync status signal.\\n- **Bottleneck**: `backlog_list(from_github=True)` returns an error response instead of cached items when the backend is transiently unavailable, breaking any agent or tool workflow that calls it explicitly.\\n- **GitHub deployments**: A transient 403 primary rate-limit causes a session-length OFFLINE instead of a bounded retry, losing up to 120s of retry headroom.\\n- **Durability gap**: Writes made while offline are silently lost \u2014 no journal, no replay, no user-visible pending-write signal.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:15:45.672938Z</sub>\\n\\n- Non-GitHub backend deployments stop seeing false OFFLINE warnings and stale-cache noise.\\n- `backlog_list(from_github=True)` degrades gracefully to cached data during transient outages instead of erroring.\\n- Transient GitHub primary rate-limits are recovered within the existing retry budget rather than triggering OFFLINE.\\n- Lifespan re-entry is idempotent: no duplicate sync tasks, no premature cancellation.\\n- Write-replay (once designed) enables true offline-first workflows: local writes survive backend unavailability and converge when connectivity returns.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:15:49.726423Z</sub>\\n\\n**Backend-aware skip (Problem 1):** When the configured backend is not GitHub (sqlite, memory, beads), `_backlog_lifespan` detects this before calling `try_start()` and skips the startup sync entirely. No `BackendUnavailableError` is raised, no OFFLINE state is set, no stale-cache warning is emitted. `SyncStatus` remains `IDLE`.\\n\\n**Read-path stale-cache fallback (Problem 2):** When `backlog_list(from_github=True)` is called and the backend raises `BackendUnavailableError`, the tool falls back to serving cached items as if `from_github=False` were passed. The response includes a warning that the backend is unavailable and results reflect the local cache. The tool does not return `{\\\"error\\\": ...}`.\\n\\n**403 primary-limit retry (Problem 3):** When GitHub returns HTTP 403 with `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0` (and no `Retry-After`), `_classify_github_exception` returns `RETRYABLE`. The sync engine enters the backoff loop rather than setting OFFLINE. The check precedes the `Retry-After` header check in the 403 branch.\\n\\n**Lifespan re-entry safety (Problem 4):** A second `_backlog_lifespan` entry while the first sync is in-flight does not create a second task. A second entry after the first sync has completed also does not create a second task. The once-per-process guard holds across all lifecycle orderings. The finally block of any re-entrant lifespan does not cancel the task started by the first entry.\\n\\n**Write-replay (design target):** When a write (groom, update, resolve) is made while the GitHub backend is unavailable, the operation is recorded in a local journal with an idempotency key, an operation type, and a target item identifier. On reconnect (OFFLINE\u2192sync transition), the journal is drained before the next full refresh. Conflict resolution reuses `_pick_entry` / `_merge_entry_bodies` from `operations.py`.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:15:53.409138Z</sub>\\n\\nFour targeted fixes to existing code, plus one new design component:\\n\\n1. **Backend-aware skip**: A check in `_backlog_lifespan` (or `_startup_sync_loop`) that queries the configured backend type before calling `try_start()`. Non-GitHub backends skip the sync path entirely.\\n\\n2. **Read-path fallback**: In `server.backlog_list`, the `BackendUnavailableError` catch branch (server.py:2148) falls back to a second `list_items(from_github=False)` call and returns cached items with a warning, instead of returning an error dict.\\n\\n3. **403 primary-limit retry**: In `sync_state._classify_github_exception`, the `_HTTP_FORBIDDEN` branch (sync_state.py:218\u2013220) checks for `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0` in addition to `Retry-After`. A 403 with rate-limit-remaining zero is `RETRYABLE`.\\n\\n4. **Lifespan re-entry guard**: The re-entry check in `_backlog_lifespan` is strengthened so that `try_start()` is not called after the first sync has completed to IDLE. The module-level `_active_startup_sync_task` reference is the discriminator: if it is not None, skip `create_task` regardless of current `SyncStatus`.\\n\\n5. **Write-replay design**: A new `WriteJournal` component (schema, storage, idempotency, ordering, drain trigger) designed during the planning phase. The journal schema, conflict policy, and OFFLINE\u2192online drain sequence are defined before any implementation. The drain calls back into existing reconciliation logic in `operations.py`.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:15:57.501482Z</sub>\\n\\n1. **Non-GitHub backend zero OFFLINE events**: When `BACKLOG_BACKEND=sqlite` (or `beads` or `memory`), the server starts without raising `BackendUnavailableError` and `SyncStatus` remains `IDLE`. Zero GitHub API calls are made during server lifespan. Testable: unit test with mocked sqlite backend; assert `SyncStatus == IDLE`, zero calls to `refresh_local_cache_from_github`.\\n\\n2. **`backlog_list(from_github=True)` serves cached items on backend unavailability**: Given a mocked `BackendUnavailableError` from `refresh_local_cache_from_github`, `backlog_list(from_github=True)` returns an `items` list (not `{\\\"error\\\": ...}`) populated from the local cache, and includes a warning string containing \\\"unavailable\\\" or \\\"stale\\\". Testable: unit test with mocked backend.\\n\\n3. **403 with `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0` is classified RETRYABLE**: Given a `GithubException` with `status=403`, `headers={\\\"x-ratelimit-remaining\\\": \\\"0\\\"}`, and no `Retry-After`, `classify_sync_error(exc)` returns `SyncErrorKind.RETRYABLE`. Testable: direct unit test on `classify_sync_error`.\\n\\n4. **403 without rate-limit header and without Retry-After is still NON_RETRYABLE**: The existing behavior for a generic 403 (no rate-limit signal, no Retry-After) is preserved. Testable: existing test case continues to pass.\\n\\n5. **Lifespan re-entry after completed sync does not create a second task**: Given a server that has completed its first startup sync (status IDLE) and `_active_startup_sync_task is not None`, a second `_backlog_lifespan` entry does not call `asyncio.create_task`. Testable: unit test with mocked `create_task`; assert call count == 1 across two lifespan entries.\\n\\n6. **Lifespan re-entry while first sync running does not create a second task and does not cancel the running task**: The existing in-flight guard (via `try_start()`) continues to hold when the first sync is still RUNNING. The finally block of the second entry does not cancel the first task. Testable: unit test.\\n\\n7. **Write-replay design doc exists before implementation**: A planning artifact (SAM task or design note) defining the journal schema, idempotency key, ordering model, and drain trigger exists and is linked from the SAM plan before any implementation task for write-replay is dispatched.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:16:05.065384Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_engine.py` \u2014 `_startup_sync_loop`, `_attempt_sync`, `_run_single_sync` |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py` \u2014 `_classify_github_exception`, `classify_sync_error`, `SyncState.try_start()` |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:1545\u20131584` \u2014 `_backlog_lifespan` |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:2131\u20132150` \u2014 `backlog_list` error handler |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:2153\u20132196` \u2014 `refresh_local_cache_from_github` |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:2379\u20132413` \u2014 `list_items` |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 `_pick_entry`, `_merge_entry_bodies` (conflict resolution) |\\n| Backlog | #2573 \u2014 Redesign backlog GitHub sync: offline-first with per-item watermarks (P1, in-progress \u2014 overlapping scope) |\\n| Backlog | #1504 \u2014 SAM server initializes TaskBackend at module import time instead of FastMCP lifespan (P2, groomed \u2014 related lifespan pattern) |\\n</div>\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:16:08.972966Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Depends on**: None for the four bug fixes (each is self-contained). Write-replay design depends on the write-through model defined in #2573 reaching a stable interface.\\n- **Blocks**: Agent workflows that call `backlog_list(from_github=True)` and expect graceful degradation on transient backend errors.\\n- **Coordination required**: If #2573 is implemented first and resolves backend-aware skip (its AC #4), that fix should not be duplicated here. This item's backend-aware fix should be treated as superseded by #2573 AC #4 if that item ships first.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:16:16.866520Z</sub>\\n\\nMedium \u2014 The four targeted fixes (Problems 1\u20134) are small and well-bounded: each touches one function in one or two files, and each has a direct unit-testable condition. The write-replay design is the wildcard: schema design, conflict policy specification, and drain-trigger integration have non-trivial design surface area. Estimated: fixes alone = Small; fixes + write-replay design = Medium; fixes + write-replay implementation = High.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:17:43.610037Z</sub>\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Bug fix (four defects) + Feature (write-replay design)\\n- Behavioral boundary: The startup sync lifecycle and the `backlog_list` read path \u2014 observable at the MCP tool call boundary (tool response content and backend-API call count) and in the `SyncStatus` state machine transitions.\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must prove that (a) non-GitHub backends do not trigger GitHub API calls during server lifespan, (b) `backlog_list(from_github=True)` returns cached items when the backend is unavailable rather than an error, (c) a GitHub 403 with `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0` enters the retry loop rather than OFFLINE, and (d) a second lifespan entry cannot cancel the in-flight sync task started by the first entry.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:16:12.860641Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Write-replay design is unspecified**: Journal schema, idempotency key strategy, ordering model, and conflict policy must be defined before implementation. This blocks only the write-replay component \u2014 the four bug fixes can proceed independently.\\n- **Scope overlap with #2573**: Without a clear scope boundary decision, there is risk of duplicating the backend-aware skip fix. Requires human input (see Questions for Human).\\n</div>\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:21:48.012848Z</sub>\\n\\n- `sync_engine.py:109-111` \u2014 `_run_single_sync` calls `operations.refresh_local_cache_from_github` unconditionally; no backend-type check.\\n- `operations.py:2193-2196` \u2014 `refresh_local_cache_from_github` raises `BackendUnavailableError` when `try_get_github()` returns `None`.\\n- `sync_state.py:249-250` \u2014 `classify_sync_error` returns `NON_RETRYABLE` for any `BackendUnavailableError`.\\n- `sync_engine.py:181-185` \u2014 `_attempt_sync` sets `SyncStatus.OFFLINE` on non-retryable.\\n- `operations.py:2412-2413` \u2014 `list_items` calls `refresh_local_cache_from_github` when `from_github=True` with no exception guard.\\n- `server.py:2131-2150` \u2014 `backlog_list` catches `BacklogError` (superclass of `BackendUnavailableError`) and returns `{\\\"error\\\": str(e), ...}` instead of cached items.\\n- `sync_state.py:218-220` \u2014 403 branch checks only `\\\"Retry-After\\\" in headers`; 403 with `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0` and no `Retry-After` returns `NON_RETRYABLE`.\\n- `server.py:1559-1577` \u2014 re-entry after completed sync passes `try_start()` (status IDLE) and creates a second task.\\n- No journal/replay/write_ahead/offline_queue/pending_writes/outbox pattern exists in `backlog_core/*.py`.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:16:01.306236Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Scope vs #2573**: Issue #2573 (P1, in-progress) explicitly covers backend-aware skip (its AC #4) and write-through durability. Should #2549 be narrowed to only the three fixes NOT covered by #2573 (read-path fallback, 403 retry classification, lifespan re-entry guard), and the write-replay design deferred to or merged into #2573?\\n- **403 detection signal**: Should the 403 primary-limit classification check `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0` only, or also check for the `x-ratelimit-used` header or the response body text `\\\"API rate limit exceeded\\\"` to be more robust?\\n- **Write-replay scope**: The item lists write-replay as a design-only deliverable. Should the planning phase produce an implementation plan and schema, or also produce a prototype that is validated against the existing reconciliation logic before full implementation is scheduled?\\n</div>\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:16:38.801857Z</sub>\\n\\n- Goal: Add four hardening behaviors to the startup-sync subsystem: backend-aware skip, read-path stale-cache fallback, 403 primary-limit retry, and lifespan re-entry safety; plus a write-replay design.\\n- Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\n- AVAILABLE:\\n  - All four defects are directly observable in source with specific line numbers.\\n  - `sync_state._classify_github_exception` logic is complete and verifiable (sync_state.py:218-223).\\n  - `_backlog_lifespan` re-entry guard is readable (server.py:1559-1584).\\n  - `list_items` / `refresh_local_cache_from_github` error propagation path is observable (operations.py:2193-2196, 2412-2413; server.py:2131-2150).\\n  - No offline write journal exists today \u2014 confirmed by grep across backlog_core/*.py.\\n  - Design intent for offline-first reads is documented in the item description.\\n- DERIVABLE:\\n  - Backend-awareness check can be derived from existing `try_get_github` / `try_start()` pattern.\\n  - Stale-cache fallback can be derived from pre-PR-2546 behavior and the `BackendUnavailableError` catch at server.py:2148.\\n  - 403 primary-limit fix is mechanically derivable from the existing `_HTTP_FORBIDDEN` branch (sync_state.py:218-220).\\n- MISSING:\\n  - Write-replay design decisions: journal schema, idempotency key strategy, ordering constraints, and conflict policy are not documented anywhere in the codebase or item. Required before any write-replay implementation.\\n  - Scope boundary with #2573 (P1, in-progress): #2573 explicitly covers backend-aware skip (its AC #4), dirty-item protection (AC #3), and awaited write-through. The extent to which #2549 should be narrowed to avoid duplicate work is not resolved.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:18:58.937272Z</sub>\\n\\n**Problem 1 \u2014 Non-GitHub backends forced OFFLINE (sync_engine.py:109-111, operations.py:2193-2196, sync_state.py:249-250, sync_engine.py:182-183):**\\n\\n1. Configure `BACKLOG_BACKEND=sqlite` (or `beads`).\\n2. Start the backlog MCP server.\\n3. `_backlog_lifespan` calls `state.try_start()` \u2192 `True` (status is IDLE at startup).\\n4. `_sync_engine._startup_sync_loop` calls `operations.refresh_local_cache_from_github` with no backend-type check.\\n5. `refresh_local_cache_from_github` calls `try_get_github()` \u2192 returns `None` for sqlite/beads.\\n6. `operations.py:2195` raises `BackendUnavailableError(\\\"GitHub unavailable: GITHUB_TOKEN not set...\\\")`.\\n7. `classify_sync_error(BackendUnavailableError)` returns `NON_RETRYABLE` (sync_state.py:249-250).\\n8. `_attempt_sync` sets `state.status = SyncStatus.OFFLINE` (sync_engine.py:182-183).\\n9. All subsequent `backlog_list` calls emit stale-cache warnings even though the sqlite backend is healthy.\\n\\n**Problem 2 \u2014 Read-path loses stale-cache fallback (operations.py:2412-2413, server.py:2131-2150):**\\n\\n1. Configure `BACKLOG_BACKEND=github` but make GitHub unreachable.\\n2. Call `backlog_list(from_github=True)`.\\n3. `operations.list_items(from_github=True)` calls `refresh_local_cache_from_github` (operations.py:2413).\\n4. `refresh_local_cache_from_github` raises `BackendUnavailableError`.\\n5. Exception propagates through `asyncio.to_thread` back to `server.backlog_list`.\\n6. Handler catches `BacklogError` (superclass of `BackendUnavailableError`) at server.py:2148.\\n7. Handler returns `{\\\"error\\\": str(e), \\\"backend\\\": ..., ...}` \u2014 no cached items are returned.\\n8. Expected: handler catches the exception, re-calls `list_items(from_github=False)`, returns cached items with a stale-cache warning.\\n\\n**Problem 3 \u2014 GitHub 403 primary rate-limit classified non-retryable (sync_state.py:218-220):**\\n\\n1. Simulate a GitHub 403 response with `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0` and NO `Retry-After` header.\\n2. `_classify_github_exception` matches `status == _HTTP_FORBIDDEN` (sync_state.py:218).\\n3. Checks `\\\"Retry-After\\\" in headers` \u2014 absent \u2192 returns `NON_RETRYABLE`.\\n4. Sync enters OFFLINE instead of bounded retry.\\n5. Expected: 403 with `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0` is a primary rate limit and should be `RETRYABLE`.\\n\\n**Problem 4 \u2014 Lifespan re-entry after first sync completes launches second task (server.py:1559-1577):**\\n\\n1. First lifespan entry: `state.try_start()` \u2192 `True`; task created, stored in `_active_startup_sync_task`.\\n2. First sync completes \u2192 `state.status = SyncStatus.IDLE`.\\n3. Second lifespan entry: `state.try_start()` checks `status == RUNNING` \u2192 `False` (status is IDLE) \u2192 returns `True` \u2192 second `asyncio.create_task` is called.\\n4. If outer lifespan's `finally` runs before inner, it cancels `_active_startup_sync_task` (now the second task), cancelling work that should still run.\\n5. If re-entry occurs while first sync is still running: the inner context's `bg_task` is set from `_active_startup_sync_task` (the outer task), so both finally blocks cancel the same task \u2014 double-cancel.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **harden startup-sync offline-first behavior: backend-aware skip, read-path stale-cache fallback, 403 primary-limit retry, lifespan re-entry, and write-replay design** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: PR #2546 bot review (chatgpt-codex-connector, 2026-06-03) + session design discussion on offline-first\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-03\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"6fea48b328224e547d0ab82271efb1bfbdb2a96781f749f1c202182199fd6b71\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPTA9vQ\"}"}